{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-143528",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that a chosen email is spam, and \n\n- let ${B}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${B}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${B}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${B}_{1}⊔{B}_{2}⊔{B}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that Alice received a total of \n\n$$\n16+10+14=40\n$$\n\nemails in all three accounts.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{1}),$ we take the number of emails in the first account that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{1})=\\frac{5}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{2}),$ we take the number of emails in the second account that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{2})=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap {B}_{3}),$ we take the number of emails in the third account that are spam $(3)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {B}_{3})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {B}_{1})+P(S\\cap {B}_{2})+P(S\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40}+\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
